      "Mixed buyer reports on mixing and grittiness"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A sensible sucralose-free casein for night-time shakes, if you blend it.",
    "specs": {
      "Protein per serving": "24 g (label)",
      "Calories": "About 110 (databases)",
      "Third-party testing": "No certification found",
      "Best for": "Before-bed protein"
    },
    "amazonUrl": "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B08Q6N8PQS"
  },
  {
    "id": "naked-nutrition-naked-whey",
    "name": "Naked Whey",
    "brand": "Naked Nutrition",
    "badge": "Alternative: One Ingredient",
    "category": "Unflavored grass-fed whey concentrate",
    "description": "Naked Whey goes one step simpler than Levels: a single ingredient, grass-fed whey concentrate, with no sweetener or flavoring. It delivers about 25 g of protein per serving in roughly 120 calories, per the label at the time of writing. Its Amazon listing title mentions NSF certification, so check NSF's database for the program and product before relying on it.",
    "pros": [
      "One ingredient, with no sweeteners or flavorings",
      "About 25 g of protein per serving, per the label",
      "Ideal for smoothies and baking"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Unflavored, so it needs blending or mixing into food",
      "Concentrate carries more lactose than an isolate"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Choose Naked over Levels if you want no sweetener at all.",
    "specs": {
      "Protein per serving": "About 25 g (label)",
      "Calories": "About 120",
      "Third-party testing": "Check NSF database",
      "Best for": "Minimal-ingredient buyers"
    }
  },
  {
    "id": "transparent-labs-grass-fed-whey-protein-isolate",
    "name": "Transparent Labs 100% Grass-Fed Whey Protein Isolate",
    "brand": "Transparent Labs",
    "badge": "Alternative: Isolate Upgrade",
    "category": "Grass-fed whey protein isolate",
    "description": "Transparent Labs is the upgrade if you like Levels' grass-fed, sucralose-free approach but want an isolate. It lists about 28 g of protein per scoop depending on flavor, per the label at the time of writing, is sweetened with stevia, and the brand publishes lab results. It costs more per tub, but the larger scoop and lower lactose suit lifters and sensitive stomachs.",
    "pros": [
      "About 28 g of protein per scoop (label, varies by flavor)",
      "Isolate, so less lactose than a concentrate",
      "Published lab results"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Premium price",
      "Stevia rather than monk fruit"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "More protein, less lactose and more documentation than Levels, for a higher price.",
    "specs": {
      "Protein per serving": "About 28 g (label)",
      "Calories": "Check label by flavor",
      "Third-party testing": "Published lab results",
      "Best for": "Lifters, lactose-sensitive buyers"
    }
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/`,
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/best-of/`,
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Levels Protein Powder Review",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/levels-protein-powder-review/`,
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is Levels protein powder good?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, for most everyday users. Levels Grass Fed Whey lists 24 g of protein per scoop from grass-fed whey concentrate, sweetened with monk fruit and free of artificial flavors, and Amazon buyers rate it highly for taste. Its gaps are no sport certification we could find and more lactose than an isolate. It is a good daily whey, not a tested-athlete product."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is Levels whey isolate or concentrate?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Levels Grass Fed Whey is a whey protein concentrate, per its Amazon listing, made from grass-fed, hormone-free dairy and cold-process microfiltered. Concentrate keeps a little more lactose, fat and carbohydrate than isolate. We did not find a Levels isolate on Amazon at the time of writing. For an isolate with grass-fed sourcing, Transparent Labs is a common alternative."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What sweetener does Levels use?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The Levels whey and casein listings name monk fruit extract as the sweetener, with no sucralose, acesulfame potassium or artificial flavors. Some buyer reviews also mention stevia, so the system may differ by flavor. If you avoid either sweetener, read the ingredient panel of the specific flavor you buy before ordering a large tub."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is Levels protein third-party tested?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "We could not find Levels in the NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Sport, Informed Choice or Clean Label Project databases at the time of writing, and the listings do not claim those certifications. We also did not find published batch test results. Drug-tested athletes should choose a certified whey instead, such as one from our isolate ranking."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How many servings are in a Levels tub?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The Amazon listings state 28 servings in the 2 lb Levels Grass Fed Whey and 71 servings in the 5 lb tub, each serving providing 24 g of protein. That implies a scoop of about 32 g. The casein is sold in 2 lb and 4 lb tubs; check the label for its serving count, as the listing details we checked did not state it."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Where can I buy Levels protein?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Levels sells mainly through its own Levels Nutrition storefront on Amazon, and the listings we checked were sold by Levels Nutrition. When we checked, the levelsnutrition.com address redirected to a domain-for-sale page rather than a store, so Amazon is the practical place to buy it at the time of writing. Avoid unknown third-party resellers."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is Levels casein good before bed?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It can be. Levels Micellar Casein lists 24 g of slow-digesting protein per scoop, and research suggests pre-sleep casein can support overnight muscle protein synthesis. But total daily protein matters more than timing for most people. Buyer feedback on its texture is mixed, so blending it or making a thicker pudding-style serving tends to work better than shaking it in water."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: SITE_NAME, url: SITE_URL },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.brand ?? product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Brand Reviews",
    tags: [
      "levels protein powder",
      "levels grass fed whey",
      "levels whey review",
      "levels protein"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Whey Protein Picks",
    link: "/best-whey-protein-powder/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker="Brand Review" badge="Label-Checked" author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Levels Protein Powder Review</Fragment>
      Levels is an Amazon-first brand selling a grass-fed whey concentrate and a micellar casein, both with 24 g of protein a scoop and monk fruit instead of sucralose. We checked what the labels promise, what is missing and what each 25 g of protein costs.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#verdict">The Short Verdict</a>
      <a href="#brand">Who Levels Is and Where It Sells</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">Our Top Picks</a>
      <a href="#whey">Levels Grass Fed Whey, Label-Checked</a>
      <a href="#casein">Levels Micellar Casein</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g of Protein</a>
      <a href="#claims">Monk Fruit, Undenatured and Other Claims</a>
      <a href="#testing">Testing, Grass-Fed and Lactose</a>
      <a href="#taste">Taste and Mixing</a>
      <a href="#who">Who Should Buy Levels, and the Alternatives</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Levels Grass Fed Whey is a solid, short-label whey concentrate that buyers rate highly for taste, at a fair mid-tier price. It is not an isolate, it carries no sport certification we could find, and its marketing claims go further than the evidence. Buy it for a clean everyday shake; skip it if you are drug-tested or lactose-sensitive.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Verdict
          </h2>

          <p>
            Levels Grass Fed Whey is a good everyday whey with an unusually short label. Each scoop lists 24 g of protein and 5.4 g of BCAAs, per the Amazon listing at the time of writing, from grass-fed, hormone-free whey protein concentrate. It is sweetened with monk fruit extract rather than sucralose or acesulfame potassium, and the flavors use ingredients such as vanilla extract and cacao rather than artificial flavoring.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buyers like it. The Vanilla Bean 2 lb listing we checked carried about 4.4 out of 5 stars across more than 7,000 ratings, and Amazon's review summary highlights taste, the clean formula and mixing. Levels has been selling on Amazon since 2017 and the whey ranks among the top sellers in its Amazon category, which is a useful signal of consistency even if it is not a quality test.
          </p>

          <p>
            The limits are clear too. It is a concentrate, so it carries more lactose and a little more fat and carbohydrate per scoop than an isolate. We could not find NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Sport or Clean Label Project certification. And some of the marketing, such as claims about naturally occurring growth factors, says more than the evidence supports. Our <a href="/best-whey-protein-powder/">best whey protein powder</a> ranking shows where it sits against the big names.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE VERDICT</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">A clean, well-liked concentrate at a fair price.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Levels is worth buying for a sucralose-free daily whey. It is not the pick for drug-tested athletes, people with lactose trouble or anyone chasing the lowest cost per gram.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            The casein is the less-reviewed half of the range, with fewer ratings and more mixed feedback on texture. It shares the whey's approach, with 24 g of protein a scoop, monk fruit and no artificial flavors, and suits people who want a slow-digesting shake before bed. Most buyers only need the whey.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="brand" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Who Levels Is and Where It Sells
          </h2>

          <p>
            Levels Nutrition is a US brand that sells mainly through its own storefront on Amazon. The first Levels whey listing dates to June 2017, per Amazon's product details, and the product is labelled as made in the USA. The listings we checked were shipped and sold by Levels Nutrition itself, which is the seller line you want to see when buying protein on Amazon.
          </p>

          <p>
            One thing worth knowing before you search for the brand directly: when we checked, the levelsnutrition.com address redirected to a domain-for-sale page rather than a store. That does not affect the Amazon listings, which were active and in stock, but it means Amazon is effectively the place to buy Levels, and the place to find label details, at the time of writing.
          </p>

          <p>
            That Amazon-first model has trade-offs. Prices can move quickly, sale pricing is common, and label information is spread across listing text and images rather than a single brand page. It also means there is less brand-published detail on sourcing and testing than you get from companies that publish certificates of analysis on their own sites. We did not find published batch test results for Levels.
          </p>

          <p>
            The range itself is focused. There is Levels Grass Fed Whey, sold in flavors including Vanilla Bean, Double Chocolate, Chocolate Peanut Butter and Strawberry, in 2 lb and 5 lb tubs; and Levels 100% Micellar Casein, in Chocolate, Vanilla Bean and Unflavored, in 2 lb and 4 lb tubs. There is no Levels isolate, plant protein or clear whey on Amazon that we could find.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Product</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Sizes</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein per scoop</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Levels Grass Fed Whey</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2 lb (28 servings), 5 lb (71 servings)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Levels 100% Micellar Casein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2 lb, 4 lb</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24 g</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Levels Range and Two Alternatives
          </h2>

          <p>
            Levels sells two proteins, a grass-fed whey concentrate and a micellar casein, in a handful of flavors and two tub sizes. The cards below cover the flagship whey, the large tub that lowers its cost per gram, the casein, and two clearly labelled alternatives. Figures are per the Amazon listings at the time of writing and can vary by flavor.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="whey" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Levels Grass Fed Whey, Label-Checked
          </h2>

          <p>
            The protein is whey protein concentrate made from grass-fed, hormone-free dairy, per the listing. Levels says each batch is cold-process microfiltered and blended with non-GMO sunflower lecithin to help it mix. The Vanilla Bean flavor uses Madagascar vanilla extract and monk fruit extract; the chocolate flavors use cacao powder. Amazon's review summary describes the vanilla formula as having six ingredients.
          </p>

          <p>
            Each scoop lists 24 g of protein and 5.4 g of BCAAs, with no added sugar. A 2 lb tub holds 28 servings, which implies a scoop of roughly 32 g, so protein makes up about three quarters of the powder by weight. That is typical for a good concentrate. Nutrition databases list a scoop at about 130 calories; check the label on your tub, as it can vary slightly by flavor.
          </p>

          <p>
            Those numbers put Levels in line with the mainstream. Gold Standard 100% Whey, for comparison, lists 24 g of protein per scoop as a blend of isolate and concentrate. The difference with Levels is not the dose but the label: no sucralose, no artificial flavors and no gums or fillers listed. If you care about that kind of label, Levels gets it with a mainstream protein dose rather than a reduced one.
          </p>

          <p>
            What the label does not offer is the extra purity of an isolate. Concentrate typically keeps more lactose, fat and carbohydrate than isolate, and the gap is a few grams per scoop rather than a different product. For most people that is fine, and some prefer the creamier taste. For people who get bloated after milk, an isolate is the safer first try, which our <a href="/whey-vs-whey-isolate/">whey vs whey isolate</a> guide explains.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Grass Fed Whey, per Scoop</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">24 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protein</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">5.4 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">BCAAs</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">0 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Added sugar</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">28</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Servings per 2 lb</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="casein" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Levels Micellar Casein
          </h2>

          <p>
            Levels 100% Micellar Casein lists 24 g of protein and 5.2 g of BCAAs per scoop with no added sugar, per the listing. It is made from hormone-free dairy, and the listing calls micellar casein the least processed form of casein. The chocolate flavor uses cacao powder and natural flavor, the vanilla uses natural vanilla flavor, and both are sweetened with monk fruit and mixed with sunflower lecithin.
          </p>

          <p>
            Note one difference from the whey: the casein listing describes the dairy as hormone-free but does not call it grass-fed. That may simply be a wording choice, but if grass-fed sourcing is the reason you buy Levels, it is the whey, not the casein, that carries the claim. Nutrition databases list a casein scoop at about 110 calories; check your tub's label.
          </p>

          <p>
            Micellar casein digests more slowly than whey, which is why it is popular as a before-bed shake. Research on pre-sleep casein suggests it can support overnight muscle protein synthesis, though total daily protein matters far more than timing for most people. Our <a href="/best-casein-protein-powder/">best casein protein powder</a> guide covers the evidence and the alternatives.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buyer feedback on the casein is less settled than on the whey. The chocolate 2 lb listing sat at about 4.3 out of 5 stars across roughly 500 ratings, and Amazon's summary reports mixed comments on mixing and texture, with some buyers finding it smooth and others gritty or thick. That is common for casein, which naturally forms a thicker shake. Using a blender, or making it into a pudding with less liquid, helps.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Cost per 25 g of Protein
          </h2>

          <p>
            At the prices we saw on Amazon at the time of writing, a 2 lb tub of Levels Grass Fed Whey worked out at roughly 2.05 to 2.10 dollars per 24 g serving, or about 2.15 dollars per 25 g of protein. The 5 lb tub brings that down to roughly 1.85 dollars per serving, or about 1.90 dollars per 25 g. Sales and Subscribe and Save discounts can move both figures noticeably.
          </p>

          <p>
            That places Levels in the mid tier for whey. It costs more per gram than large tubs of mainstream whey concentrate, which our <a href="/best-cheap-protein-powder/">best cheap protein powder</a> guide puts around 1 to 1.50 dollars per 25 g at typical prices. It costs less than many premium grass-fed isolates. You are paying a modest premium for the grass-fed sourcing and the sucralose-free label rather than for more protein.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
