        <div id="claims" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Monk Fruit, Undenatured and Other Claims
          </h2>

          <p>
            The strongest part of Levels' pitch is the sweetener. The listings name monk fruit extract as the sweetener, with no sucralose, acesulfame potassium or sugar alcohols. Monk fruit is a natural, zero-calorie sweetener that many people find cleaner tasting than stevia, though some buyer reviews mention stevia as well, so the system may differ between flavors. Check the panel on your flavor if you avoid either one.
          </p>

          <p>
            The weaker part is the language around processing. Levels describes its whey as undenatured, cold-processed and rich in naturally occurring growth factors. Gentle processing can preserve more of whey's native protein structure, but there is no good evidence that this makes a meaningful difference to muscle growth or health for people drinking a shake. Once whey hits your stomach, digestion breaks it down regardless.
          </p>

          <p>
            The no-hormones claim deserves context too. Levels says it uses hormone-free dairy, which usually refers to milk from cows not treated with the synthetic growth hormone rbST. That is a reasonable preference, but it is a common practice among US dairy suppliers rather than a unique feature, and all milk naturally contains small amounts of hormones. It is not a reason on its own to pay more.
          </p>

          <p>
            None of this makes Levels a bad product. The protein dose is real, the label is short and the sweetener choice is genuinely appealing to people who react to sucralose. The point is simply to buy it for those concrete reasons, not for the wellness language around them. The same caution applies to every grass-fed brand, and our <a href="/best-clean-protein-powder/">best clean protein powder</a> guide explains what clean claims can and cannot prove.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Testing, Grass-Fed and Lactose
          </h2>

          <p>
            We could not find Levels in the NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Sport, Informed Choice or Clean Label Project databases at the time of writing, and the Amazon listings we checked do not claim any of those certifications. We also did not find published certificates of analysis for individual batches. That leaves buyers relying on the brand's own quality control.
          </p>

          <p>
            For drug-tested athletes, that is a dealbreaker regardless of how good the label looks. Only third-party sport certifications screen batches for banned substances and stand behind the result. For everyone else, whey from dairy generally tests lower for heavy metals than many plant proteins, and nothing we found points to a problem with Levels. Our <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">is protein powder safe</a> guide covers what each certification checks.
          </p>

          <p>
            The grass-fed claim is the brand's own. Levels does not name a third-party grass-fed certification on the listings we checked. That is common: most grass-fed whey brands rely on supplier statements, and the term has no single, strict US standard for dairy. If you want grass-fed sourcing with published lab testing, Transparent Labs is the alternative to look at, as its reviews on our site explain.
          </p>

          <p>
            On lactose, Levels is a concentrate, so it contains more than an isolate. Many people with mild lactose intolerance handle a single scoop of concentrate without issue, but some do not. If you have had stomach trouble with milk-based shakes, try a single tub first, take it with food, or choose an isolate. Anyone with a diagnosed milk allergy should avoid whey and casein entirely, and check with a doctor or dietitian.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="taste" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Taste and Mixing
          </h2>

          <p>
            We have not tasted Levels, so this section reports buyer feedback. Taste is the most-mentioned topic in the whey's Amazon reviews, and Amazon's breakdown at the time of writing counted roughly 1,230 positive mentions against about 340 negative ones. The common theme is that it tastes natural and not overly sweet, which makes sense for a monk fruit formula without artificial flavoring.
          </p>

          <p>
            That lower sweetness cuts both ways. Buyers used to dessert-style wheys sweetened with sucralose sometimes describe Levels as bland. If you like intense flavors, the chocolate peanut butter and double chocolate flavors are the more forgiving choices, and mixing with milk rather than water adds body. If you mainly use protein in smoothies, oats or baking, a mild flavor is an advantage, not a flaw.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mixing gets good marks for the whey, helped by the sunflower lecithin, and Amazon's summary notes buyers using it in baking and smoothies as well as shakes. The casein draws more mixed comments, which is typical of casein's naturally thicker texture. A blender or a shaker with a mixing ball makes a noticeable difference for either product.
          </p>

          <p>
            If flavor is the main thing you are shopping for, our <a href="/best-tasting-protein-powder/">best tasting protein powder</a> guide covers the brands buyers rate highest for taste. Levels does well there among sucralose-free options, but the dessert-flavor specialists win if sweetness is what you want.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Who Should Buy Levels, and the Alternatives
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buy Levels Grass Fed Whey if you want a daily whey with a full 24 g dose, no sucralose and a short ingredient list, and you are happy with a concentrate at a mid-tier price. It suits people who drink one shake a day, people who bake with protein and anyone who has found sucralose-sweetened wheys too sweet. The 5 lb tub is the value choice once you have found a flavor.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it if you compete in a drug-tested sport, because there is no sport certification. Skip it if you are lactose-sensitive, because an isolate will usually suit you better. And skip it if your only goal is the cheapest protein, since mainstream whey concentrates cost less per gram. Pregnant readers, people with kidney disease and anyone on medication should check with a doctor or registered dietitian before adding protein supplements.
          </p>

          <p>
            The closest alternative is Naked Whey, which strips the label down further to a single ingredient, grass-fed whey concentrate, with no sweetener at all. It suits people who blend their shakes or bake. Our <a href="/naked-nutrition-protein-powder-review/">Naked Nutrition protein powder review</a> compares its range in detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            The upgrade alternative is Transparent Labs Grass-Fed Whey Isolate, which delivers more protein per scoop, less lactose and published lab results, at a higher price. If you want grass-fed whey with more documentation behind it, start there; our <a href="/transparent-labs-protein-powder-review/">Transparent Labs protein powder review</a> covers it, and the <a href="/best-whey-protein-isolate/">best whey protein isolate</a> ranking compares it with other isolates.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating grass-fed as a testing claim</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Grass-fed describes how the cows were raised, per the brand. It says nothing about banned substances, heavy metals or label accuracy, and Levels names no third-party grass-fed certification. If you need verified testing, look for NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport, which Levels does not carry that we could find.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying concentrate with lactose trouble</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Levels is a whey concentrate, which keeps more lactose than an isolate. Many people handle it fine, but if milk-based shakes have bloated you before, start with an isolate such as Transparent Labs, or try a single 2 lb tub of Levels with food before committing to the 5 lb size.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying for undenatured marketing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Claims about undenatured whey and preserved growth factors sound impressive, but there is no good evidence they change results for people drinking a shake. Judge Levels on its 24 g dose, short label and monk fruit sweetener, which are real advantages, and ignore the rest.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Starting with the 5 lb tub</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The 5 lb tub cuts the cost per 25 g of protein from about 2.15 to about 1.90 dollars at typical prices, but 71 servings is a lot of a flavor you might not like. Lower-sweetness formulas divide opinion, so buy a 2 lb tub first, then reorder in the large size.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying from unknown resellers</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Levels sells through its own Amazon storefront, and the listings we checked were sold by Levels Nutrition. Third-party sellers can offer old or mishandled stock. Check the seller line under the price, look at the best-by date on arrival and return anything with a broken seal.
              </p>
            </div>
