          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Levels protein powder good?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes, for most everyday users. Levels Grass Fed Whey lists 24 g of protein per scoop from grass-fed whey concentrate, sweetened with monk fruit and free of artificial flavors, and Amazon buyers rate it highly for taste. Its gaps are no sport certification we could find and more lactose than an isolate. It is a good daily whey, not a tested-athlete product.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Levels whey isolate or concentrate?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Levels Grass Fed Whey is a whey protein concentrate, per its Amazon listing, made from grass-fed, hormone-free dairy and cold-process microfiltered. Concentrate keeps a little more lactose, fat and carbohydrate than isolate. We did not find a Levels isolate on Amazon at the time of writing. For an isolate with grass-fed sourcing, Transparent Labs is a common alternative.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What sweetener does Levels use?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  The Levels whey and casein listings name monk fruit extract as the sweetener, with no sucralose, acesulfame potassium or artificial flavors. Some buyer reviews also mention stevia, so the system may differ by flavor. If you avoid either sweetener, read the ingredient panel of the specific flavor you buy before ordering a large tub.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Levels protein third-party tested?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  We could not find Levels in the NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Sport, Informed Choice or Clean Label Project databases at the time of writing, and the listings do not claim those certifications. We also did not find published batch test results. Drug-tested athletes should choose a certified whey instead, such as one from our isolate ranking.
                </p>
